##Proposal

Part I: Introduction and Research Question
Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) has a five-year survival rate of about 9%, largely
because early disease is asymptomatic and most patients are diagnosed after surgery is no longer
curative. Debernardi et al. evaluated a non-invasive urinary panel of LYVE1, REG1B, and TFF1,
combined with age and creatinine in a logistic regression model [1]. However, the study reports
uncertainty only at the group level, as confidence intervals around AUC, sensitivity, and
specificity. It does not assess calibration, per-patient confidence, or which patients are
misclassified. This matters because performance drops in the hardest comparisons: the AUC falls
to 0.871 for benign disease vs. early PDAC and to 0.818 for chronic pancreatitis vs. early PDAC
[1]. Chronic pancreatitis is both a risk factor for PDAC and a condition that can resemble it. An
“elevated” result given without any indication of confidence could lead to unnecessary invasive
follow-up, while a confident but wrong “normal” result could delay diagnosis. The study also
had few stage I–IIA samples. We will test whether prediction uncertainty can flag these
unreliable classifications.
Research Question
Can predictive uncertainty estimates identify unreliable classifications of early-stage pancreatic
cancer from urinary biomarkers, particularly when distinguishing cancer from benign
hepatobiliary disease?
●
Justification: This question goes beyond summary statistics: it requires building and
validating predictive models, estimating per-sample uncertainty, and testing whether
uncertainty predicts errors, none of which the original study reports. It is feasible because
the data being used is public and small enough for repeated model fitting. Lastly, it is
important because a screening tool that cannot signal when it is unsure could lead to
unnecessary invasive procedures or missed early cancers.
Part II: Data Sources
We have accessed and assessed the study’s public S1 Table, journal.pmed.1003489.s009.xlsx,
through its supporting information [1]. It contains 590 sample records: 183 controls, 208 benign
disease samples, and 199 PDAC samples. Restricting cancer cases to stages I-II leaves 493
records, including 102 cancer samples. Only 16 are stage I, so stage I alone would be too small
for the primary analysis.
Age, creatinine, LYVE1, REG1B, and TFF1 are complete for all 493 eligible records. Sample
IDs are unique, although the file lacks a separate participant identifier. CA19-9 and REG1A are
missing in 213 and 257 eligible records, respectively. The diagnosis and stage will define
