Part III: Proposed Methods
We will train a logistic regression model using age, creatinine, LYVE1, REG1B, and TFF1 to
distinguish stage I–II pancreatic cancer from healthy controls and benign disease. Concentration
measurements will be log-transformed, and predictors will be standardized using training data
only. We will evaluate performance using stratified five-fold cross-validation, reporting AUROC,
sensitivity, specificity, and balanced accuracy, with the classification threshold chosen within
each training fold. We will assess calibration with a calibration curve.
For each held-out sample, we will estimate uncertainty from the variation in predicted
probabilities across 200 bootstrap models trained within each training fold. We will test whether
higher uncertainty identifies incorrect predictions, and whether flagging uncertain cases reduces
the error rate among the remaining predictions, while reporting how many cancer cases are
flagged. We will compare this approach with proximity to the classification threshold, use a
random forest as a secondary model, and examine errors among benign cases, particularly
chronic pancreatitis, and performance across collection sites.
Course Topics
1. Logistic regression. We will reproduce the published logistic regression model on age,
creatinine, LYVE1, REG1B, and TFF1. Concepts: log transformation, standardization, maximum
likelihood estimation. Justification: This matches the original PancRISK model, so our results
can be compared directly with the published AUCs. It also serves as the base model we refit on
bootstrap samples to estimate uncertainty. Stage: data cleaning and data analysis.
2. Data splitting and classification evaluation. We will use stratified five-fold cross-validation,
fitting all preprocessing on training folds only in order to prevent data leakage. Concepts:
stratified splitting, confusion matrices, AUROC, sensitivity, specificity, balanced accuracy, and
threshold selection. Justification: cancer cases are a minority (102 of 493), so stratification and
imbalance-aware metrics are needed for honest evaluation. Stage: data analysis.
3. Ensembles and uncertainty. We will estimate per-sample uncertainty from the spread of
predictions across 200 bootstrap models and compare it with distance to the classification
threshold. We will also train a random forest as a secondary model. Concepts: bootstrapping and
bagging, random forests, epistemic uncertainty, ensemble disagreement, calibration curves.
Justification: This directly answers our research question. The random forest is a nonlinear
model, so it tests whether the uncertainty findings hold beyond logistic regression. Stage: data
analysis and final report.
